      "notes": "All claims are well-supported by their quoted evidence. Tier assignments are appropriate: E3, E4, E5 are measurable baselines describing the existing legal landscape; E8 is correctly marked projected (proposals, not enacted law); E31 is measurable (current government stance). The 'measurable' claim using E4 (waronwant.org, impactpolicies.org, unseenuk.org) relies on advocacy sources but is also backed by commonslibrary.parliament.uk and publications.parliament.uk via E5, so it is not solely advocacy-sourced. No tier blurring detected. Direction 'improves' is well-grounded: the gap between the current voluntary baseline and a mandatory regime with civil liability is large and well-documented, and no substantive claims point the other way. Magnitude 'moderate' is justified given enforcement uncertainty. Time horizon 'this-parliament' is reasonable for legislative introduction but is inherently optimistic for full effect \u2014 not blocking given the caveat in plain text. No party-blind issues; rationale references institutional endorsement (JCHR) rather than party identity. No lever leakage; the verdict focuses on equal treatment and access to justice for affected workers. Minor note: some sources (ethicaltrade.org, antislavery.org, unseenuk.org, waronwant.org) are advocacy organisations and are labelled as 'media' rather than 'advocacy' in the sources list \u2014 this is a metadata inconsistency but does not affect the verdict's evidentiary basis given corroboration from parliamentary sources."
